<!--
    Swift runtime-metadata recovery (`option swiftmetadata on|off`).

    Issue: a stripped Swift binary names nothing. On the Toyota iOS app
    (84 MB arm64, 314,038 functions, 304,309 of them `sub_<addr>`) the
    `__swift5_*` reflection sections still describe 14,677 types, 1,317
    protocols and 30,938 conformances, and point at 57,147 of the stripped
    functions: every type's metadata accessor, every class vtable slot's
    implementation, and every protocol witness.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement, the Swift metadata pass
    (`kuna-analysis/src/analyzers/swift/`), beside the ObjC metadata pass.

    P0 assertion: `option swiftmetadata on|off` (default off).

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. Both passes
    clear it and load the real fixture `swift_metadata_elf`: an x86-64 ELF
    executable built with swiftc 6.3 and `llvm-strip` (strip all), so no
    symbol survives for its own functions (see the fixtures README for the
    source and the pinned VMAs from the unstripped build).

    Pass 1 (`option swiftmetadata off`, the default) reproduces the BUG: the
    metadata accessor at 0x30b0, the vtable method at 0x2fe0 and the witness
    at 0x2ab0 are `sub_30b0`, `sub_2fe0`, `sub_2ab0`. Pass 2 (`option
    swiftmetadata on`) is the FIX: the same functions are
    `metafix.Point.type_metadata_accessor`, `metafix.Animal.vtable#4.method`
    and `<metafix.Square:Swift.Hashable>.hash(into:)`.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
